  h1="Connecting advertising to booked work."
  sub="Punch List Pros | Home repair and improvement"
  ctaHead="Can you follow your marketing through to the sale?"
  ctaBody="Find out where the connection breaks between attention, inquiries, and the work your business books."
>
  <p class="cs-lead">In August 2026, Punch List Pros’ Google Ads reporting showed approximately $5.20 in booked-job value for each dollar of media spend, based on closed-job values uploaded into the account.</p>
  <p>That month, the account recorded 14 attributed sales.</p>
  <p>For a home-service business, connecting those numbers matters. A click is a starting point. An inquiry is an opportunity. Booked work is closer to what the owner needs the marketing to produce.</p>

  <h2>Giving each channel a clear role</h2>
  <p>Our engagement with Punch List Pros began in April 2026. The program combines search advertising with Meta advertising, intent-based audiences, and email follow-up.</p>
  <p>Search reaches people asking for a service. Service-specific landing pages give them information related to the work they need and a way to make an inquiry.</p>
  <p>Alongside search, intent data feeds advertising audiences and a client-specific email drip campaign. Those emails offer additional paths to the website or Google Business Profile, allowing prospects to explore the business and return when they are ready to act.</p>
  <p>The program creates several opportunities to begin a conversation. Someone searching for handyman help today and someone still considering a project can enter through different channels.</p>

  <h2>Measuring the inquiries</h2>
  <p>From April 1 through September 9, 2026, the Meta program recorded 97 lead-form submissions at approximately $29 per submission. That cost includes the custom-intent traffic campaigns as well as the lead-form advertising.</p>
  <p>These were recorded form submissions, rather than website clicks labeled as leads. They are also a different measure from the Google Ads booked-work figure. Keeping the two distinct makes the contribution of each channel easier to evaluate.</p>
  <p>The website records showed another useful pattern. From June 1 through September 9, the Columbia handyman search landing page recorded a 16.2% form-conversion rate on 788 paid-search sessions. A page that speaks directly to the service being sought gives the visitor a clear next step.</p>

  <h2>Following the numbers through the sales process</h2>
  <p>The account review looked beyond the platform’s headline conversion value. Inspection, quote, and sale stages each carried values, which meant adding them together overstated the value of completed business.</p>
  <p>The booked-work calculation uses the sale-stage values. It does not count an inspection, a quote, and a sale as three separate jobs.</p>
  <p>That distinction is central to the work. Advertising decisions need consistent definitions: what counts as an inquiry, what became a sale, and which costs are included in the return calculation.</p>
  <p>The August figure is a return on Google media spend. It is not profit, and it does not include agency fees or the cost of fulfilling the jobs. It is a concrete way to connect the advertising investment to attributed business recorded in the account.</p>
</CaseStudyPage>
